#
# D-138 — A DERIVED GUARD IS ONLY AS TOTAL AS ITS LEAST-DERIVED AXIS.
# D-134 — AND ITS MEMBERSHIP MUST BE DERIVED OVER THE PACKAGE, NOT ONE MODULE.
#
# D-138. `_run_artifact_problems` derived ONE of its three membership axes and
# hand-bound the other two, inside the fix written to make hand-binding
# unrepresentable:
#
#   candidates = sorted(p for p in fdir.glob("*") if p.is_file())   [DERIVED]
#   manifest = fdir / "castings" / "manifest.json"                  [HAND-NAMED]
#   decoder = _ARTIFACT_DECODERS.get(candidate.suffix.lower())      [HAND-KEPT
#   if decoder is None: continue                                     TABLE, and
#                                                                    a SILENT
#                                                                    skip]
#
# Consequence 1: artifacts foundry writes ITSELF were unguarded. `spawns.log`
# and `handoffs.jsonl` are in every live run dir and outside the two-entry
# table, so a corrupt one returned NO problem, Foundry-Next named nothing, and
# Foundry-Liveness raised UnicodeDecodeError across the MCP boundary.
# Consequence 2: every subdirectory artifact except the one hand-named manifest
# was not a member at all.
#
# The first table is that claim driven. Rows are DERIVED (every file the run dir
# holds, at any depth) so the table cannot quietly stop covering something, and
# three rules judge each row: the original `*.json` glob, the D-129 rule, and
# this one. Ten rows, six of them invisible to BOTH earlier rules.
#
# All three axes are derived now: rglob (not glob, and no hand-named
# subdirectory), and a type decided by the artifact rather than by a suffix
# table. `.json` keeps a STRICTER rung on top of the text floor, which is an
# extension point and not a gate: a suffix absent from it falls THROUGH to the
# floor instead of out of the scan.
#
# THE SECOND TABLE IS THE PART THAT WAS GOT WRONG FIRST, kept because the
# correction is the point. A content sniff ("a NUL byte in the first 8000 means
# binary, skip it") was written, and it read a text artifact whose CORRUPTION
# contained a NUL as a binary file to be passed over — silently losing the very
# artifact the guard exists to name. Driven: a `.trace-clean-at` overwritten
# with b"\xe9\x00..." came back clean.
#
# So the table was inverted into an EXEMPTION list: recognised binary is
# skipped, everything else must decode, and an unrecognised type is REPORTED.
# That direction is what D-138 asks for in as many words — a new artifact type
# announces itself rather than being skipped — and over-reporting is
# recoverable where under-reporting is D-098, D-129 and D-138 themselves.
#
# It also has to not cry wolf, and the first cut did: measured against the live
# archives, `.pyc` files under __pycache__ produced 13 reported artifacts in
# thunder-viper and 317 in grand-vulture. A guard that refuses on those does not
# harden the mandatory handshake, it kills it. CPython's magic is
# `<2-byte version> + b"\r\n"` for every version, which is what the second row
# below exempts — asserted against `importlib.util.MAGIC_NUMBER` rather than
# typed, with a second version planted beside it. Both real archives now report
# 0 problems over 181 and 825 files, in 48ms and 153ms.
#
# The third row is D-140's residual reached from here: a DIRECTORY named
# `state.json` passed the old `is_file()` membership filter, so the guard saw
# nothing and the write then raised IsADirectoryError. A path OCCUPYING an
# artifact's name is the guard's business whatever kind of thing it is. The
# same filter is removed from `forge_spec.py#_planning_guard`, which is where
# D-140 filed it.
#
# D-134. D-132's nested-shape validator was bound inside foundry_spawn.py, its
# membership derived over THAT MODULE's functions rather than over every reader
# of castings/manifest.json in the package. Five readers in two other modules
# indexed the same records behind a top-rung-only guard, and `castings: "nope"`
# met `.get()` and raised AttributeError out of Foundry-Next — the mandatory
# handshake before EVERY phase transition, so the most-travelled door in the
# tool surface, not an edge case.
#
# All five now reach D-132's OWN validator, not a private isinstance chain:
#   foundry_orchestrator#_check_sight_required, #_trace_skip_check, #foundry_gate
#     — via a LAZY in-function import, because foundry_spawn imports this module
#       at module top and a module-scope import would close the graph;
#   foundry_validate#foundry_validate_castings, #_fingerprint_inputs
#     — module-top, since validate -> spawn -> orchestrator has no cycle.
# And the record rung is bound at `_artifact_guard` too, which every MCP entry
# point already calls, so Foundry-Next names the file in `corrupt_artifacts`
# the way every other artifact refusal on this surface does.
#
# RESIDUAL, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT SILENT: casting 4's package-wide test
# `test_every_manifest_record_reader_in_the_package_establishes_the_shape` still
# reports one offender, `evidence.py#_append_to_manifest_evidence_provenance`.
# evidence.py is casting 3's file this cycle and this casting's grant covers
# only its two D-137 read sites, so it is reported here and in the completion
# report rather than reached across a write boundary.
